Carafe: a thread that finds no free place among the 96 waits up to 5 s for one instead of being refused.
Games start short loading and shader threads in bursts; when such a burst meets the limit, the creator now waits
in 2 ms steps for a thread to end, so the burst is served a little later instead of failing. DXVK, for one, ends
the process when it is refused a thread. The wait applies only while the program runs and not while it is being
closed; the runtime's start-up, which fills the limit on purpose, is refused at once as before. [BUDGET] counts
the waits, those that ended without a place and the time spent, and the first waits are logged as [THREAD] lines.
LGPL-2.1-or-later, as the file it changes.

